assessments:
  - discriminant: mcq
    value:
      id: 413-mcq-01
      prompt: "A national supermarket chain runs a recruitment campaign for store managers and includes in its job advert that applicants 'should be at the start of their career to fit the dynamic culture'. Two applicants in their 50s with extensive retail management experience are not invited to interview despite meeting the formal criteria. Which Act has the supermarket most clearly breached?"
      options:
        - The Computer Misuse Act 1990
        - The Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR
        - The Equality Act 2010
        - The Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988
      correct_index: 2
      feedback_correct: |
        Correct. The Equality Act 2010 protects against direct
        discrimination on grounds of age (section 13, age being one of
        the nine protected characteristics). The job advert wording acts
        as a less-favourable selection criterion against older
        applicants, and the decision not to invite the over-50
        applicants is a direct consequence. The supermarket may attempt
        an "objective justification" defence (uniquely available for age
        among the protected characteristics) but the burden is on the
        employer to show the criterion is a proportionate means of
        achieving a legitimate aim, and "dynamic culture" is unlikely
        to meet that test.
      feedback_incorrect: |
        The answer is the Equality Act 2010. The CMA, DPA and CDPA are
        not engaged here. The Act lists age as one of the nine protected
        characteristics, and the job-advert wording plus the
        non-invitation of the over-50 applicants is a textbook direct
        age discrimination scenario under section 13.
      difficulty: foundation
  - discriminant: nei
    value:
      id: 413-nei-01
      stimulus: |
        A bank launches a digital-only current account aimed at younger
        customers. The only way to open an account is to complete a
        live video-call identity check, which requires uninterrupted
        use of a smartphone camera, a stable internet connection and
        clear lighting. The bank does not offer any alternative
        identity-check route. Several older customers without
        smartphones, and several customers with severe motor
        impairments who cannot reliably hold a phone steady, are unable
        to complete the process.
      question: |
        Name the legislation the bank is at risk of breaching, explain
        which forms of discrimination are engaged, and describe the
        impact for the customers and for the bank.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          The Equality Act 2010. The strongest answers name two distinct
          routes: indirect discrimination (section 19) on the grounds of
          age and disability, and failure to make reasonable adjustments
          (sections 20-22) on the grounds of disability. Credit one
          route fully named.
        explain_expected: |
          The requirement to complete a smartphone-only video-call
          identity check is a "provision, criterion or practice" (PCP)
          that applies neutrally on its face but puts older customers
          and customers with motor impairments at a particular
          disadvantage compared to younger non-disabled customers. To
          defend this as lawful, the bank must show the PCP is a
          proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim. Verifying
          identity is a legitimate aim, but proportionality is
          questionable because cheaper and well-established alternative
          checks exist (in-branch, document upload, supported video
          call). Separately, under sections 20-22 the bank owes a
          positive duty to disabled customers to make reasonable
          adjustments where a PCP puts them at substantial disadvantage,
          and a refusal to offer any alternative process is very
          unlikely to satisfy that duty.
        impact_expected: |
          For affected customers, the impact is exclusion from the
          banking service, loss of access to a product they are
          entitled to access on equal terms, the effort of pursuing a
          complaint or claim, and the wider message that the financial
          product is not designed for them. For the bank, affected
          individuals can bring a discrimination claim in the County
          Court under section 114, with available remedies including
          declarations, injunctions to change the process, and damages
          for injury to feelings. The Equality and Human Rights
          Commission can investigate. There is reputational damage in
          the press, regulatory attention from the Financial Conduct
          Authority on inclusive design grounds, and the cost of
          rebuilding the onboarding flow to include accessible
          alternatives.
      exemplar_answer: |
        The bank is at clear risk of breaching the Equality Act 2010
        through two distinct routes. The first is indirect
        discrimination under section 19. The requirement to complete a
        live smartphone video-call identity check is a "provision,
        criterion or practice" that applies neutrally but puts older
        customers and customers with motor impairments at a particular
        disadvantage compared to younger non-disabled customers,
        engaging the age and disability protected characteristics. The
        bank can only defend this if it shows the PCP is a proportionate
        means of achieving a legitimate aim, and although verifying
        identity is a legitimate aim, proportionality fails because
        well-established alternative checks (in-branch verification,
        postal document checks) exist and would meet the same aim
        without the disadvantage. The second route is the disability
        duty under sections 20-22, which positively requires the bank
        to make reasonable adjustments where its standard PCP puts
        disabled customers at substantial disadvantage. Refusing to
        offer any alternative process is unlikely to satisfy that
        duty. The impact for customers is exclusion from a financial
        product they are entitled to access on equal terms, the time
        and effort of pursuing a complaint, and the deterrent effect on
        engaging with the service at all. The impact for the bank is
        exposure to County Court claims under section 114 (with damages
        for injury to feelings available), Equality and Human Rights
        Commission investigation, reputational harm, regulatory
        scrutiny on inclusive-design grounds, and the cost of
        rebuilding the onboarding flow.
drill:
  - id: 413-drill-01
    cue: The number of protected characteristics listed in section 4 of the Equality Act 2010, and the foundational structure on which the rest of the Act rests.
    answer: Nine (age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, sexual orientation)
  - id: 413-drill-02
    cue: Treating a person less favourably because of a protected characteristic, under section 13 of the Equality Act 2010.
    answer: Direct discrimination
  - id: 413-drill-03
    cue: Applying a provision, criterion or practice that applies neutrally on its face but puts those sharing a protected characteristic at a particular disadvantage, and which cannot be objectively justified.
    answer: Indirect discrimination (Equality Act 2010, section 19)
  - id: 413-drill-04
    cue: Unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic that has the purpose or effect of violating a person's dignity or creating an intimidating, hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive environment.
    answer: Harassment (Equality Act 2010, section 26)
  - id: 413-drill-05
    cue: Subjecting a person to a detriment because they have done a protected act (such as raising a discrimination complaint or giving evidence in a discrimination case).
    answer: Victimisation (Equality Act 2010, section 27)
  - id: 413-drill-06
    cue: The disability-specific positive duty to make reasonable adjustments where a provision, a physical feature or the absence of an auxiliary aid puts a disabled person at a substantial disadvantage.
    answer: The reasonable adjustments duty (Equality Act 2010, sections 20-22)
  - id: 413-drill-07
    cue: The defence available against an indirect-discrimination claim when the employer or service provider can show the PCP is necessary for a real and serious purpose.
    answer: Objective justification (a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim)
  - id: 413-drill-08
    cue: Treating a person less favourably because of a protected characteristic that the person is perceived to have, even if they do not actually have it, or because of a protected characteristic of someone they are associated with.
    answer: Discrimination by perception or by association
  - id: 413-drill-09
    cue: The UK regulator that promotes and enforces the Equality Act 2010 and can carry out investigations and issue unlawful-act notices.
    answer: The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC)
  - id: 413-drill-10
    cue: The court route for discrimination claims in goods, facilities and services cases (as distinct from employment cases, which go to the Employment Tribunal).
    answer: The County Court, under section 114 of the Equality Act 2010
---

Six panels and a closer. The Equality Act 2010 catches decisions and
processes that disadvantage people on the grounds of a protected
characteristic. Most Paper 1 slips happen when the bias is implicit
in a technical choice rather than explicit in a decision-maker's words.

{% panels %}

{% panel num="01_NINE" eyebrow="WHERE THE LAW APPLIES" headline="The [nine] protected characteristics." illo="eq-nine" %}
Section 4 of the Act lists nine. Every Equality answer starts here. The first move in any scenario is to identify which characteristic is engaged.

- age
- disability
- gender reassignment
- marriage and civil partnership
- pregnancy and maternity
- race (includes colour, nationality, ethnic origin)
- religion or belief (includes absence of belief)
- sex
- sexual orientation

{% takeaway %}
Name the characteristic first. If more than one is engaged, name the most central and flag the others.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="02_FORMS" eyebrow="PROHIBITED CONDUCT" headline="[Four ways] the Act bites." illo="eq-forms" %}
{% term-card %}
s13 DIRECT :: less favourable treatment because of a characteristic
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s19 INDIRECT :: a neutral PCP that disadvantages a group
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s26 HARASSMENT :: unwanted conduct that violates dignity
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s27 VICTIMISATION :: detriment for raising a protected complaint
{% /term-card %}

Direct also covers perception (the victim is thought to have the characteristic) and association (the victim is connected to someone who has it).

{% takeaway %}
Naming the form is the Explain mark. Most digital scenarios are indirect, but harassment and victimisation appear in workplace fact patterns.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="03_PCP" eyebrow="PROVISION, CRITERION OR PRACTICE" headline="Surface-neutral rules can still [discriminate]." illo="eq-pcp" %}
{% def %}
A **PCP** is any rule, criterion or practice that applies to everyone on its face but puts a group sharing a protected characteristic at a particular disadvantage. The bias is in the effect, not in the wording.
{% /def %}

Voice-recognition that fails regional accents. Recruitment AI trained on a historically male workforce. Smartphone-only identity checks that exclude older customers. Each is a PCP. Each is potentially unlawful under section 19 unless the party applying it can objectively justify it.

{% takeaway %}
Spell out the PCP. Name the disadvantaged group. The strongest Paper 1 answers refuse to accept the defendant's framing that the rule is "neutral".
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="04_ADJUSTMENTS" eyebrow="ANTICIPATORY DUTY" headline="Disability demands [active accommodation]." illo="eq-adjustments" %}
{% def %}
Sections 20 to 22 impose a positive duty to make **reasonable adjustments** where a provision, a physical feature or the absence of an auxiliary aid puts a disabled person at a substantial disadvantage. In services, the duty is anticipatory: owed before any disabled customer arrives, so the system must be designed with accessibility built in.
{% /def %}

For digital products the duty surfaces as alternative input methods (keyboard alongside mouse, text alongside voice), screen-reader support, contrast and font-scaling, and a non-digital fallback for users who cannot complete the digital flow at all. Failure to adjust is itself unlawful discrimination under section 21.

{% takeaway %}
"We will fix it if someone complains" does not satisfy the anticipatory duty. The fix has to be ready before the disadvantage hits.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="05_JUSTIFY" eyebrow="OBJECTIVE JUSTIFICATION" headline="When discrimination can be [defended]." illo="eq-justify" %}
{% def %}
A PCP that causes indirect discrimination can be lawful if the party applying it can show the PCP is a **proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim**. The aim must be real and serious. The PCP must be no more discriminatory than necessary to achieve it.
{% /def %}

The burden sits on the party applying the PCP. Convenience almost never meets the test. Cost alone almost never meets the test. The same defence is uniquely available for direct age discrimination, which is the one exception to the rule that direct discrimination cannot be justified.

{% takeaway %}
If a cheaper or less-restrictive alternative exists and would meet the same aim, the defence fails. Identify the alternative on the facts.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="06_ENFORCE" eyebrow="ENFORCEMENT" headline="Who [enforces] the Act." illo="eq-enforce" %}
The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) is the regulator. It investigates, issues unlawful-act notices and enters into agreements with bodies in breach.

{% cloud %}
Routes: {% chip %}EHRC{% /chip %} {% chip %}Employment Tribunal{% /chip %} {% chip %}County Court s114{% /chip %} {% chip %}Judicial Review (PSED){% /chip %}
{% /cloud %}

Individuals bring their own claims. Employment claims go to the Employment Tribunal. Services and goods claims go to the County Court under section 114. Remedies include declarations, injunctions, orders to take specific steps and damages including damages for injury to feelings. There is no statutory cap on Equality Act damages.

{% takeaway %}
The Public Sector Equality Duty (s149) is the basis for judicial review when public bodies procure or build digital systems without an equality impact assessment.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% /panels %}

{% closer line="name the characteristic. [then] name the form." body="Working with an Equality scenario in Paper 1 comes down to two moves, in this order." %}
{% step num="STEP 01" head="Name the characteristic" %}
Identify which of the nine is engaged. If more than one is in play, name the most central and flag the others. The Name mark goes to the Act and the characteristic together.
{% /step %}
{% step num="STEP 02" head="Name the form, then apply" %}
Pin the conduct to s13 direct, s19 indirect, s26 harassment, s27 victimisation or s21 failure to make reasonable adjustments. State the elements of that section. Tie each element to a fact in the scenario. Assess any objective-justification defence on its merits.
{% /step %}
{% /closer %}
